with_skill.response:
Use a generation token to make the newest query the owner. Clear the subscription field **before** awaiting cancellation, then check ownership before assigning a listener. Disposal must invalidate pending continuations too.

Applying the dart-concurrency skill, keep this state on the object that owns the search:

```dart
StreamSubscription<SearchResult>? _subscription;
int _generation = 0;
bool _disposed = false;

Future<void> search(String query) async {
  if (_disposed) return;

  final generation = ++_generation;
  final previous = _subscription;
  _subscription = null;

  // Cancellation failures propagate to the caller.
  await previous?.cancel();

  if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;

  _subscription = repository.search(query).listen(
    (result) {
      if (!_disposed && generation == _generation) {
        emit(result);
      }
    },
    onError: (Object error, StackTrace stackTrace) {
      if (!_disposed && generation == _generation) {
        emitError(error, stackTrace);
      }
    },
  );
}

Future<void> close() async {
  _disposed = true;
  ++_generation;

  final previous = _subscription;
  _subscription = null;
  await previous?.cancel();
}
```

The repository produces results; this object owns cancellation and forwards only current results to its consumer. Callers should await or explicitly handle failures from `search()` and `close()`. If this object owns an output controller, close it during teardown as well.

Verify with controlled cancellation futures: start query A while cancellation is pending, start B, then release A’s cancellation. Only B should install a listener. Also test normal results, stream and cancellation failures, stale emissions, and disposal during cancellation. Run static analysis; these checks haven’t been run here.
